WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.820
In the last video, you and I looked at this
demo here where we shine linearly polarized

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.710
light through a tube full of sugar water and
we saw how it rather mysteriously results

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.630
in these coloured diagonal stripes.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.960
There I walked through the general outline
for an explanation keeping track of what questions

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.960
still need to be answered, namely; why does
sugar water twist the polarization direction

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.180
of light?

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:28.000
Why does that twisting rate depend on the
colour of the light and why even if you understand

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.250
that this twist is happening, would you see
any evidence of it when viewing the tube from

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.480
the side with no additional polarizing filters?

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.960
Here I'd like to begin with the very fundamental
idea of what light is and show how the answers

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:48.430
to these questions can emerge from an extremely
minimal set of assumptions.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.090
In some sense, the fundamental question of
electricity and magnetism is how the position

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.690
and motion of one charged particle influences
that of another.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.750
For example, one of the first things you learn,
say in a high school physics class is that

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.830
charges with the same sign tend to repel each
other.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.620
And the strength of this force depends a lot
on the distance between them.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:13.790
If your charges are close, that repulsive
force is very strong, but it decays very rapidly

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.450
as these particles go away from each other.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.650
Specificaly, here's how you might see this
written down as an equation known as Coulomb's

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:21.650
law.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.440
The force is proportional to the charge of
both of the particles where it's common to

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.660
use the letter Q. There are some constants
in there which for our purposes we can just

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.759
think of as one big proportionality constant
and the important fact is that you've got

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:38.409
this 1 divided by r^2 term where r is the
distance between them.

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:43.549
So, for example, if the distance between them
increases by a factor of three, the force

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:48.040
that they're applying to each other goes down
by a factor of nine.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.680
Another way you might see a law like this
written down is to focus on just one charged

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.930
particle and then say for every point in space,
if there was a second charge there, what force

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:03.180
would this first charge be applying to that
second one?

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.799
And instead of describing a force per se,
you might see this written describing what's

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:11.500
known as the "Electric Field", which is just
a way of saying what force would be applied

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.580
to a unit charge and in this context the word
"Field" means there is a value associated

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.489
with every single point in space.

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:23.580
So, the way I have it written here, it depends
on a little vector R which would be the vector

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.750
from our charge to a given point in space
and the direction of this field at all points

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.530
is in the same direction as R.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:36.140
I bring up Coulomb’s law to emphasize that
it's not the full story.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.790
There are other ways that charges influence
each other.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.000
For example, here's a phenomenon that this
law alone could not explain.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.510
If you wiggle one charge up and down, then
after a little bit of a delay, a second charge

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:52.330
some distance to its right will be induced
to wiggle up and down as well.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.860
We can write down a second law, which you
might think of as a correction term to be

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.980
added to Coulomb's law that describes what's
going on here.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.250
Suppose at some point in time, T 0, that first
charge is accelerating.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:08.489
Then I'll let time play forward but leave
on the screen a kind of ghost of that particle

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:14.160
indicating where it was and how it was accelerating
at this time T 0.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.799
After a certain delay, this causes a force
on the second charge and the equation describing

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:22.270
force looks something like this...

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.340
So again, it's proportional to the charge
of both of the particles.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.220
And once more, a common way to write it involves
this pile of constants that you don't really

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.629
need to worry about.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:35.780
The important factor I want you to notice
is how the force also depends on the distance

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.480
between the particles.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.340
But instead of decaying in proportion to R
squared, it only decays in proportion to R.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:48.439
So, over long distances, this is the force
that dominates and Coulomb's law is negligible.

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:53.620
And then finally, it depends on the acceleration
of that first particle but it's not the acceleration

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.930
of that particle at the current time, it's
whatever that acceleration was at some time

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.420
in the past.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.360
How far in the past depends on the distance
between the particles and the speed of light

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.420
denoted with C. The way to think about it
is that any form of influence can't propagate

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.099
any faster than this speed C.

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:16.780
In fact, a more accurate description of Coulomb’s
law would also involve a delay term like this...

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.590
Again, the intuitive way to read this equation
is that wiggling a charge in one location

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:26.780
after some delay causes a wiggle to a second
charge in another location.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.620
And actually the way I have it written right
now is a little bit wrong - instead of the

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:35.460
acceleration vector here, I should really
be writing something like a perp indicating

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:41.020
the component of that acceleration vector
which is perpendicular to the line drawn between

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.020
the two charges.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:46.000
In other words, when you wiggle that first
charge, the direction that the second charge

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.360
wiggles is always perpendicular to line between
them and the amount that it wiggles gets weaker

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.350
and weaker when that line between them is
more lined up with the initial acceleration.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.610
As before, this is something you might see
written down in a way that describes a component

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.789
of the electric field caused by just one charge.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:10.190
Again, that means what force would be applied
to a second charge at all possible different

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.669
points in space.

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:17.009
This component of the field is only ever non
zero when our first charge is moving somehow

00:05:17.009 --> 00:05:19.389
- when it has an acceleration vector on it.

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:24.669
And because of this delay term, the effects
on this field tend to radiate away from the

00:05:24.669 --> 00:05:25.669
charge.

00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:27.949
This is why I'm writing it down with the subscript
"rad".

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:32.389
This is the component of the electric field
that will radiate away from a given charge.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:37.449
For instance, when the charge is oscillating
up and down, you get these propagating waves

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:41.480
and for many of the vector fields, I'll be
showing the intensity of the field as illustrated

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.390
with the opacity of each little vector.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.949
This radiating influence is light.

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:52.130
Or more generally, electromagnetic radiation,
including things like radio waves and X-rays

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.919
and all that good stuff.

00:05:54.919 --> 00:05:58.830
As a side note, you sometimes see this propagation
described a very different way that puts the

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:03.330
fields front and center using what are known
as "Maxwell's Equations".

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:07.821
For our purposes, I want to focus just on
this one law and show just how far it can

00:06:07.821 --> 00:06:11.630
take us when it comes to intuitions for light.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:16.289
For the animations I'm about to show, all
I've really done is encoded in this one law

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:20.410
which tells us what should this component
of the electric fields be at every point in

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:26.440
space as determined by the history of accelerations
of a particular charge.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:31.280
For example, if I set that charge oscillating
up and down in the Z direction and illustrate

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.630
this component of the electric field everywhere
on the XY plane, you see these circular propagations

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:41.150
of equal strength in all directions.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:46.139
It's a little easier to think about if we
focus on just one axis like the X axis.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:50.009
And at first when I made this animation, I
assumed that there was some kind of bug because

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:53.060
near the charge it just looks crooked and
wrong.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.879
But when you think about it, this is actually
what you should expect.

00:06:55.879 --> 00:07:00.470
Because, remember, each one of these vectors
is supposed to be perpendicular to the line

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:06.569
drawn between that point and where the charge
WAS at some point in the past.

00:07:06.569 --> 00:07:10.169
At points that are far enough away from the
charge, which is where this component of the

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:14.900
field is what dominates anyway, the wiggling
in the field is essentially parallel to the

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.199
wiggling in the charge.

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:20.009
Which is why when we think about light waves,
we are safe to think about the wiggling direction

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:23.220
as being perpendicular to the propagation
direction.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.840
Like I said, this propagation for just one
charge is equally strong in all of the directions

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.009
perpendicular to its wiggling and really I
should emphasize that the propagation does

00:07:32.009 --> 00:07:37.389
happen in all directions of three dimensional
space.

00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:41.069
It's maybe a little busy to try to illustrate
the full three dimensional vector field on

00:07:41.069 --> 00:07:42.090
screen like this.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:46.509
So, it's clarifying if we just focus on say
the XZ plane.

00:07:46.509 --> 00:07:49.759
Notice how the waves here are strongest in
the X direction.

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:52.070
But it still does propagate in all other directions.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:56.639
It's just that that propagation gets weaker
in directions that are more aligned with the

00:07:56.639 --> 00:07:58.229
original wiggling.

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:04.730
At the extreme, the only place where there's
no propagation is in the Z axis.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:08.900
Because our law has this 1 divided by R in
it, the strength of the wave caused by just

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:14.650
one particle does decay as you go further
away in proportion to 1 over R. But notice

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:20.280
what happens if I take a whole row of charges,
say oriented along the Y axis and I have them

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.300
all start wiggling up and down in the Z direction
and I illustrate the combined effects that

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:30.100
all of them have on this component of the
electric field.

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:35.599
The effects of all these charges interfere
deconstructively along the Y direction but

00:08:35.599 --> 00:08:39.019
they interfere constructively along the X
direction.

00:08:39.019 --> 00:08:43.930
This is what it looks like for a beam of light
to be concentrated along just one dimension.

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:50.089
So, if you were to focus on the field just
along the X axis, instead of decaying in proportion

00:08:50.089 --> 00:08:55.630
to 1 over R, this combined effect decays much
more gently.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:09:00.000
In the extreme, you can get something arbitrarily
close to those pure sine wave propagations

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.430
we were illustrating earlier if at some distance
away you have a large number of charges oscillating

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.500
in sync with each other like this.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.089
One thing that's worth emphasizing when you
see light illustrated with a sine wave like

00:09:11.089 --> 00:09:16.560
this is that even that wave is being drawn
in two or three dimensions, it's only describing

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.829
the electric field along a one dimensional
line, namely the base of all those vectors.

00:09:22.829 --> 00:09:26.540
It's just that to draw the vectors, you have
to venture off of that line.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:30.830
Great, so one of the last important things
to highlight before we get back to the sugar

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:32.880
water is polarization.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.519
And everything I've been showing, the driving
charge is just oscillating along a single

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:41.950
direction, like the Z axis, and this causes
linearly polarized light.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:43.519
But it doesn't have to happen like that.

00:09:43.519 --> 00:09:48.950
For example, if I set the charge rotating
in a little circle along the YZ plane, meaning

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:53.490
its acceleration vector is also rotating in
a little circle, notice what the field looks

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:54.890
like.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:58.630
This is known aptly enough as circularly polarized
light.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:03.100
Honestly, it's easiest to think about for
just one point of the electric field.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.941
What it means for light to be circularly polarized
is that at that point, the electric field

00:10:07.941 --> 00:10:11.110
vector is just rotating in a circle.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:15.270
People often find circular polarization a
little confusing and I suspect part of the

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:19.950
reason for that is that it's hard to illustrate
with static diagram, but also it's a little

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:22.769
confusing when you try to think about the
full electric field.

00:10:22.769 --> 00:10:27.640
For example, here's what the field looks like
on the XY plane when I set that little charge

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.279
rotating in a circle.

00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:35.149
It's certainly very beautiful, I could look
at this all day but you can understand why

00:10:35.149 --> 00:10:37.589
it might feel a little confusing.

00:10:37.589 --> 00:10:41.310
The very last thing I'll mention is that while
everything here is a classical description

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:45.060
of light, the important points still hold
up in quantum mechanics.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.910
You still have propagating waves, there's
still polarization that can be either linear

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.200
or circular.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.340
The main difference with quantum mechanics
is that the energy in this wave doesn't scale

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:59.370
up and down continuously like you might expect,
it comes in discrete little steps.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:03.750
I have another video goes into more detail
but for our purposes, thinking about it classically

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.720
is fine.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.880
Part of the reason I wanted to go through
that is because, frankly it's just very fun

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:12.430
to animate and I like an excuse for a fundamental
lesson.

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.170
But now let's turn back to our demo and see
how we can build up an intuition for some

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:20.690
of our key questions, starting from this very
basic premise that shaking a charge in one

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:24.230
location causes a shake to another charge
a little bit later.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:27.730
And let's start by actually skipping ahead
to question number three; Why do we see the

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:34.470
diagonal stripes?

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:38.370
To think about this, you need to imagine an
observer to the side of the tube and then

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:43.740
for a particular pure colour, say red, if
the observer looks in the tube and sees that

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:47.810
colour, it's because light of that colour
has bounced off something at that point in

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:51.860
the tube and then propagated towards the eye
of the observer.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:56.380
Sometimes when people talk about light bouncing
off of things, the implied mental image is

00:11:56.380 --> 00:12:01.450
something like a projectile ricocheting off
of some object heading off in some random

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:02.450
direction.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:06.279
But the better mental image to hold in your
mind is that when the propagating light waves

00:12:06.279 --> 00:12:11.260
caused by some wiggling charge reach some
second charge causing it to wiggle, that secondary

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.540
wiggle results in its own propagation.

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:18.570
And for the animation on screen, that propagation
goes back to the first charge which itself

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:22.829
causes a propagation towards the second and
this is what it looks like in a very simplified

00:12:22.829 --> 00:12:27.340
situation for light to bounce back and forth
between 2 charges.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:32.100
If you have some concentrated beam of polarized
light interacting with some charge causing

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:37.199
it to wiggle up and down, then these resulting
second order propagations are most strong

00:12:37.199 --> 00:12:41.610
in the directions perpendicular to the direction
of polarization.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:45.370
In some sense, you could think of light as
bouncing off of that charge but the important

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:48.350
point is that it doesn't bounce in all directions
equally.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:52.980
It's strongest perpendicular to the wiggle
direction but gets weaker in all of the other

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.139
directions.

00:12:54.139 --> 00:12:56.540
So, think about our setup.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:00.970
And for a particular frequency of light, how
likely it is that an observer looking at a

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:03.769
particular point in the tube will see that
light.

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:08.380
Again, the key phenomenon with sugar water,
which we have yet to explain, is that the

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:13.010
polarization direction is slowly getting twisted
as it goes down the tube.

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:17.180
So, suppose the observer was looking at a
point like this one where the polarization

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:21.720
direction happens to be straight up and down,
then the second order propagations resulting

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:26.410
from wiggling charges at that point are most
strong along the plane where the observer

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:27.410
is.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:31.139
So the amount of red that they see at that
point would look stronger.

00:13:31.139 --> 00:13:35.410
By contrast, if they were looking at a different
point in the tube like this one where the

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:39.560
wiggling direction is closer to being parallel
to the line of sight, then the direction where

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:43.250
the scattering is strongest is not at all
aligned with the observer and the amount of

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:47.029
red they see is only going to be very weak.

00:13:47.029 --> 00:13:50.870
And looking at our actual physical setup,
if we first pass the light through a filter

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:55.210
showing only the red, we see exactly this
effect in action.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:14:00.060
As you scan your eyes along the tube, the
intensity of red that you see goes from being

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:06.519
high to being low where it's almost black,
back to being high again.

00:14:06.519 --> 00:14:10.500
As an analogy, imagine there was a ribbon
going down the tube always aligned with the

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:15.740
polarization direction for this color, then
putting yourself in the shoes of the observer,

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:19.220
when you look at points where the ribbon appears
very thin, you're going to see very little

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:20.300
red light.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:24.040
Whereas if you scan your eyes over the points
where the ribbon appears thicker, you're going

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.029
to see more red light.

00:14:26.029 --> 00:14:29.700
One thing that's nice about this is that if
we try it for various different colors, you

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:34.540
can actually see how the twisting rates are
different for each one of the colors.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:37.970
Notice with red light, the distance between
where it appears brightest and where it appears

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:40.519
darkest is relatively long.

00:14:40.519 --> 00:14:44.389
Whereas if you look down the colours of the
rainbow, that distance between the brightest

00:14:44.389 --> 00:14:48.100
point and the darkest point gets lower and
lower.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:54.269
So, what you're seeing in effect is how red
light twists slowly whereas light waves with

00:14:54.269 --> 00:15:01.750
higher frequencies get twisted more aggressively.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:06.440
But still, you might wonder why the boundaries
between light and dark points appear diagonal.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:11.020
Why is it that in addition to having variation
as you scan your eyes from left to right,

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:16.030
there's also variation as you scan your eyes
from the top of the tube to the bottom.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:21.579
This has less to do with what's going on in
the tube and more to do with a matter of perspective.

00:15:21.579 --> 00:15:25.470
Take a moment to think about many different
parallel beams of light ranging from the top

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:26.680
of the tube to the bottom.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:31.100
At the beginning, all of these light waves
are wiggling up and down and as you pass through

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:35.529
the tube and the effects of the sugar water
somehow twists these directions because they

00:15:35.529 --> 00:15:39.029
are all passing through the same amount of
sugar, they are getting twisted by the same

00:15:39.029 --> 00:15:40.029
amounts.

00:15:40.029 --> 00:15:44.720
So, at all points, the polarization of these
waves are parallel to each other.

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:48.829
If you are the observer and you look at the
top most point here, its wiggling direction

00:15:48.829 --> 00:15:51.310
is essentially parallel to the line of sight.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:54.820
So the light scattering from that point is
basically not going to reach your eyes at

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:55.820
all.

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:56.870
It should appear black.

00:15:56.870 --> 00:16:00.850
But if you scan your eyes down the tube, the
angle between the line of sight and the wiggling

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:05.230
direction changes and so there will be at
least some component of red light scattering

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:06.319
towards the eye.

00:16:06.319 --> 00:16:11.470
So, as you scan your eyes from top to bottom,
the amount of a particular color you see might

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:15.000
vary, say from dark to light.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.920
The full demo that has white light is basically
a combination of all these pure colour patterns

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:24.120
that go from light to dark to light with diagonal
boundaries between the intense points and

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:25.340
the weak points.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:31.440
Hence why you see diagonal boundaries between
the colours inside the tube.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:36.000
And now at last let's turn to the heart of
the matter and try to explain why interactions

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:40.080
with sugar would make light twist like this
in the first place.

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:44.110
It's related to the idea that light seems
to slow down as it passes through a given

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:45.110
medium.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:49.199
For example, if you look at the crests of
a light wave as it goes into water, the crests

00:16:49.199 --> 00:16:54.510
through the water are travelling about 1.33
times slower than the crest of that wave would

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.460
travel in a vacuum.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:59.779
This number is what's called the "Index of
Refraction" for water.

00:16:59.779 --> 00:17:04.530
In a bit, what I'd like to show is how this
index of refraction can be explained by analyzing

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:09.240
how the initial light wave shakes all the
charges in the material and how the resulting

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:14.260
second order propagations super impose with
that original light wave.

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:18.920
For right now I'll just say that the interactions
with each layer of the material ends up having

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.750
the effect of slightly shifting back the phase
of the wave.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:27.870
And on the whole, this gives the overall appearance
that that wave moves slower as it passes through

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:31.220
the material.

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:35.789
Skipping ahead to what's going on with sugar,
the relevant property of sucrose here is that

00:17:35.789 --> 00:17:38.350
it's what's called a Chiral molecule.

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.120
Meaning it's fundamentally different from
its mirror image.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:45.100
You could never reorient it in space to become
identical to its mirror image.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.549
It's like a left hand or a right hand.

00:17:47.549 --> 00:17:50.789
Or another much simpler example of a chiral
shape is a spiral.

00:17:50.789 --> 00:17:56.409
If I take this right handed spiral, then its
mirror image is a left handed spiral and no

00:17:56.409 --> 00:18:00.480
matter how you try to rotate and reorient
that first one, it will never become identical

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:04.090
to the second.

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:08.450
What's going on then is that the presence
of a chiral molecule in the water like this

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:13.900
introduces an asymmetry when it comes to interactions
with light, specifically circularly polarized

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:15.140
light.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:20.640
It turns out that the amount this chiral molecule
slows down, say left handed circularly polarized

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:25.300
light, is different from the amount that it
slows down right handed circularly polarized

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.300
light.

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:29.440
Effectively, there is not one index of refraction
but two.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:33.300
Now, you might say that seems irrelevant to
our setup.

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:37.820
Since we are very deliberately shining in
linearly polarized light, there is no circularly

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:42.690
polarized light but actually there is a sense
in which linearly polarized light is equal

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:46.740
parts left handed and right handed circularly
polarized light.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:52.510
Here, focus your attention on just one vector
in this wave wiggling straight up and down,

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:56.390
which is to say polarized in the Z direction.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:19:01.310
Notice how it's possible to express this vector
as a sum of two rotating vectors.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:08.220
One of them rotating at a constant rate counterclockwise
and the other one rotating clockwise.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:14.040
Adding them together, tip to tail, results
in a vector oscillating on a line.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:18.309
In this case, it's a vertical line but that
direction can change based on the phase of

00:19:18.309 --> 00:19:20.120
the two vectors we're adding together.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:24.360
Here, let me throw up a couple labels to keep
track of how much each one of those two vectors

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:29.320
has rotated in total and then every now and
then, I'm going to slow down that first vector

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:34.570
a little bit and I want you to notice what
happens to their sum.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:41.700
Well, every time I slow it down, effectively
knocking back its phase a little bit, it causes

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:45.860
the linearly wiggling sum to wiggle in a slightly
different direction.

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:51.960
So, if the circularly polarized light wave
represented by that left vector gets slowed

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:56.580
down a little bit every time it runs across
a sugar molecule or at least slow down more

00:19:56.580 --> 00:20:01.590
than its oppositely rotating counterpart would,
the effect on the sum is to slowly rotate

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:04.290
the direction of linear polarization.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:08.580
And hence, as you look at slices further and
further down the tube, the polarization direction

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:13.370
does indeed get twisted the way we were describing
earlier representing how the composite effects

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:17.809
with many many many different sugar molecules
are slightly different for left handed light

00:20:17.809 --> 00:20:20.470
than they are for right handed light.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:24.260
As a nice way to test whether you understood
everything up to this point, see if just by

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:29.340
looking at the direction of the diagonal slices
on our tube, you can deduce which kind of

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:31.490
light the sugar is slowing down more.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:36.220
Left handed light or right handed light.

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:40.809
I'll call this a partial answer to our question
number one because it still leaves us wondering

00:20:40.809 --> 00:20:45.110
why there is an index of refraction in the
first place and how exactly it might depend

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:49.130
on the polarization of the light not just
the material it's passing through.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:54.690
Also, like I said at the start, a robust enough
intuition here should also answer for us why

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:58.030
the strength of this effect would depend on
the frequency of the light.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:02.929
At this point, I think we've covered quite
enough for one video, so I'll pull out a discussion

00:21:02.929 --> 00:21:06.379
covering the origins of the index of refraction
to a separate video.

